---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/lib/site';
import SubscribeForm from '@/components/SubscribeForm.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`About ${SITE.title} and the people who write in the margins.`}
>
  <article class="fade-up">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">
      About
    </h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Marginalia is a space for the intellectually curious — people who read
        not just to absorb, but to start a conversation. We believe the best
        ideas aren't found in the headlines. They're hiding in the questions,
        the contradictions, and the small, quiet thoughts you jot down in the
        margins.
      </p>

      <p>
        Our goal is to challenge the main story, question what everyone takes
        for granted, and uncover the gems that others miss. Think of this as
        your invitation to pull up a chair and write in the margins with us.
      </p>

      <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
      <p>
        Longer essays that resist the hot take. Some technical, some personal,
        all written with the assumption that you have a quiet hour to read them.
        We're not interested in being first to a story; we're interested in being
        the ones still worth reading when the story is over. Expect detours into
        software, design, books, attention, and the strange places where those
        subjects overlap with the rest of life.
      </p>

      <p>
        We publish when there's something worth saying, not on a schedule. The
        <a href={SITE.social.rss}>RSS feed</a> is the most reliable way to know
        when something new is up — no algorithms, no inbox weight, just the
        words when they're ready.
      </p>

      <h2>The margins, explained</h2>
      <p>
        The name comes from the practice of writing in the margins of books —
        the small, sometimes illegible notes a reader leaves for themselves
        when a sentence lands and they don't want to forget it. Marginalia are
        the part of a book that wasn't written by the author, but that the book
        wouldn't be the same without. That's what we're after: not the polished
        argument, but the question that opened it up.
      </p>

      <h2>Who's writing</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>{SITE.author}</strong> — {SITE.bio}
      </p>

      <p>
        You can find us on <a href={SITE.social.twitter} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Twitter</a>{' '}
        and <a href={SITE.social.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>, or write
        directly at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. We read
        everything, even when we can't always reply — and we're always
        interested in a real question, even if it doesn't have a tidy answer.
      </p>

      <h2 id="subscribe">Subscribe</h2>
      <p>
        If you'd like new essays to arrive in your inbox, subscribe below.
        No spam, no algorithms, no scheduled cadence we can't keep.
      </p>
      <div class="my-6">
        <SubscribeForm />
      </div>

      <h2>Colophon</h2>
      <p>
        This site is built with <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astro</a>,
        set in <a href="https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Lora" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lora</a>{' '}
        for the body and <a href="https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inter</a>{' '}
        for the interface. The design is deliberately plain — words first,
        everything else second. The source is on{' '}
        <a href={SITE.social.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>{' '}
        if you want to see how it's put together.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
